// TEST: Idx Card Dependent Bound (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3999
// ============================================================================
// THE SAME-RECORD DEPENDENT-BOUNDS BOUNDARY, pinned as it actually stands.
//
// `l_out`'s bound references an EARLIER FIELD (`l1`) — the shape of the
// triangle inequality, and the single most natural thing to want to write
// about a coupling. On an ordinary constrained struct this is SHIPPED and
// legal:
// corpus structs/019 constructs exactly this record, because at CONSTRUCTION
// the earlier fields have values and the bound is just another conjunct.
//
// As an INDEX TYPE it is refused, and for a reason worth being precise about.
// The box must be known BEFORE enumeration begins; a bound that names another
// field has no value until a cell is chosen, so the fence's static evaluation
// of the bound fails on the free name:
//
//   struct Triangle, field 'l_out': min bound is not static —
//   Static evaluation: undefined variable 'l1'
//
// This is deferral §8.5 of the plan, and the plan is explicit that it is an
// EFFICIENCY deferral rather than a semantics gap: given ABSOLUTE bounds on
// `l_out`, a dependent bound desugars into an ordinary conjunct and the
// solution set is enumerated correctly — just over a larger box, filtered,
// instead of over tight per-cell heads. So the capability is reachable today
// by writing the absolute bound and letting the conjunct do the work, and what
// remains deferred is only the tight-heads optimization (and, in Coq, a
// dependent-heads variant of the ck arrow — deferral §8.8).
//
// The test exists so that if dependent bounds ever DO become box grammar, this
// file fails and forces the deferral to be closed deliberately rather than
// discovered.
// ============================================================================
static struct Triangle {
    l1:    Int<min=0, max=2>,
    l2:    Int<min=0, max=2>,
    l_out: Int<min=l1, max=4>
}

let static CARD = idx_card(Triangle)
